title: Why tags are a forest
tags: [[Hierarchical tags]]
kind: rationale
aliases: tag-unification
summary: A tag's hierarchy is a tree_ref, as a file's is, so the query language, the tree panel and the forest's guarantees serve tags unchanged.

Until August 2026 there were two tag conventions side by side: `mf tag` kept the
hierarchy in a string `name` (`musique/jazz`, split on `/` by the client) with plural
ref fields `tags`/`negative_tags`, while the shipped schema and the GUI placed a tag with
a `tree_ref` `path` and pointed at it with `tag`. Everything was unified on the second
(`6f05319`, `fc1524c`), for what the tree gives for free:

* the query language already follows trees — `path =>* "musique"` is a tag and its whole subtree, `path = "musique/jazz"` exactly one node ([[Following references]], [[Field aspects]]);
* the forest guarantees one position per tag, no cycle, and a parent that exists ([[TreeRef forest]], [[Referential integrity of a forest]]);
* renaming or moving a tag is one write to one node, and every path under it follows;
* the [[treeref panel]] browses and edits the vocabulary as it does folders.

The field names became singular at the same time — `tag`, `negative_tag`,
`mixed_tag` — since each row holds one tag. Data written with the old convention is not
converted: `mf tag` does not see it.
